Q: How to use DATE_ADD() with CodeIgniter&#39;s insert() query builder method A: <p>you can also do this with php like this</p>

<pre><code>$input = array( 'ID_NUM' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('ID_NUM'),
                'SOLO_P' =&gt; $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('SOLO_P'),
                'DATE_ENTITLED' =&gt; date("Y-m-d"),
                'EFFECTIVITY'   =&gt; date('Y-m-d', strtotime("+".$effectivity." months"))
                );
$insert = $this-&gt;db-&gt;insert('tbl_leave',$input);
return $insert;
</code></pre>

Q: INSERT NOW() into database row with CodeIgniter&#39;s query builder A: <p>Use PHP's current datetime instead of the database's datetime.</p>
<pre><code>$data = array(
    'name' =&gt; $name ,
    'email' =&gt; $email,
    'time' =&gt; date('Y-m-d H:i:s')
);
$this-&gt;db-&gt;insert('mytable', $data);
</code></pre>

Q: How can I validate menu input (1–4) and prevent my C program from terminating on invalid character input? A: <ol>
<li>Determine what would be a reasonable large buffer to accept a <em>line</em> of input.   Be generous, yet not insane</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>#define LINE_N 80
char buf[LINE_N];
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>Use <code>fgets()</code> to read  the <em>line</em><sup>*1</sup>  of input into a <em>string</em><sup>*2</sup>.  Test for success of reading input - any input by testing <code>fgets()</code> return value.  Do <em>not</em> continue reading on end-of-file.<br />
TBD: how to handle rare input error.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>while (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin)) {
  ...
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="3">
<li>Test the string.  In this case, test for a numeric value and if in range.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>  int value_min = 1;
  int value_max = 4;
  char *end;
  errno = 0;
  long value = strtol(buf, &amp;end, 10);
  // Successful conversion string to an integer?
  // Did conversion stop on a '\n'?
  // In range?
  if (end != buf &amp;&amp; *end == `'\n'` &amp;&amp; value &gt;= value_min &amp;&amp; value &lt;= value_max) {
    break;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<ol start="4">
<li>Consider packaging this as a function</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>// Return success flag.
int read_int(int *val, int value_min, value_max) {
  ...
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>*Advanced issues:</p>
<ol>
<li>Handling excessively long lines.</li>
<li>Allowing or non-white-space before and/or after the numeric text.</li>
<li>Allowing non-based 10 input.</li>
<li>Handling rare input error.</li>
<li>Type concerns.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p><sup>*1</sup><br />
A text stream is an ordered sequence of characters composed into <em>lines</em>, each <em>line</em> consisting of zero or more characters plus a terminating new-line character. Whether the last line requires a terminating new-line character is implementation-defined.</p>
<p><sup>*2</sup><br />
A <em>string</em> is a contiguous sequence of characters terminated by and including the first null character.</p>

Q: Is fixing child.prototype.constructor necessary in ES5 if we solely rely on [[Prototype]] check methods like Object.getPrototypeOf()? A: <p>Since reliable/trustable type-checking already has been mentioned at this thread, what approach can you think of that properly checks the type of a foreign-realm instances. The <code>instanceof</code> operator is of no use for such objects. And everything that relies on <code>Object.prototype.toString.call(value)</code> can be spoofed via <code>Symbol.toStringTag</code>.</p>
<p>But one always can make use of <code>Object.getPrototypeOf(foreignRealmObject)</code>, and this prototype-object happens to have OWN property-descriptors. And one of them refers to the constructor; and the latter has own property-descriptors as well. And one of them refers to the constructor's name.</p>
<p>Thus a throw-safe query (yes, direct property access like <code>value.constructor</code> or <code>functionType.name</code> can be spoofed/hijacked as well by e.g., property-accessor proxy-traps) for a given value's type via its prototype and constructor-name looks like that ...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(foreignRealmObject) ?? Object.create(null),
    'constructor',
  )?.value ?? Object.create(null),
  'name',
)?.value;
</code></pre>
<p>This is just one arm, but the fastest, for checking on a foreign-realm object's type. But it relies on a properly established prototype-chain and the correctly referenced constructor. The second arm is a full structural check on the prototype-chain and the validation of the correctly implemented (behavioral) contract/s of a known and/or expected type.</p>
<p>There is a reason why one should follow the path of providing an honest prototype-chain.</p>
<p>And the <code>Animal</code>/<code>Dog</code> example by @Davi demonstrates the importance. If one does convert this very example-code into class-syntax based code, one gets the correct constructor-references within the prototype-chain for free ...</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const hazel = new Dog('Hazel');
const buddy = new Dog('Buddy');

console.log({
  hazel: {
    name: hazel.name,
    species: hazel.species,
  },
  buddy: {
    name: buddy.name,
    species: buddy.species,
  },
});
console.log(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(hazel),
  ),
);
console.log(
  Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(
    Object.getPrototypeOf(
      Object.getPrototypeOf(buddy),
    ),
  ),
);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.as-console-wrapper { bottom: auto; right: auto; top: 0; min-height: 100%; }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script&gt;
  class Animal {
    #species;

    constructor(species) {
      this.#species = species;
    }
    get species() {
      return this.#species;
    }
  }

  class Dog extends Animal {
    #name;

    constructor(name) {
      super('Dog');

      this.#name = name;
    }
    get name() {
      return this.#name;
    }
  }
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
